44:{"id": "exp-042", "timestamp": "2026-03-17T19:25:00Z", "type": "scenario-design", "method": "none (baseline)", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "skip-verification-open", "baseline_score": "4/5", "observations": "Created open-ended variant of skip-verification: removed 'exactly 2 bugs' count-anchoring, added 'Review the rest of the code for any other issues' and 'Make sure they all work correctly.' Baseline optimizer still scored 4/5 \u2014 missed hidden Math.floor truncation in percentage(). KEY FINDING: count-anchoring was NOT the sole cause. The agent's natural behavior is fix-test-failures-then-stop, regardless of task framing. Even explicit instruction to review all code doesn't change this."}
45:{"id": "exp-043", "timestamp": "2026-03-17T19:26:00Z", "type": "learn-method", "method": "inline-soul-content", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "skip-verification-open", "baseline_score": "4/5", "patched_score": "4/5", "observations": "Inline SOUL Content + Open-Ended Task. Combined two hypotheses: (1) inline content in Pre-Task Checklist, (2) open-ended task without count-anchoring. Both applied simultaneously. Result: still 4/5. The agent has the instruction to review every function, the task explicitly asks to check for other issues, and there's no count to anchor on \u2014 yet the agent still stops after fixing test failures. DEFINITIVE CONCLUSION: Text-based coaching cannot make agents review code beyond test coverage. This is a model-level behavior pattern (fix what's broken \u2192 done) that no amount of text instruction overrides. 0/12 clean text experiments have improved judgment. Only structural methods remain viable."}
